**Recursion IS a stack**. Every time a function calls itself, the current execution context (local variables, return address) is pushed onto the call stack. When the function returns, that frame is popped. The maximum stack depth equals the maximum recursion depth.

This means any recursive algorithm can be rewritten iteratively using an explicit stack. The iterative version replaces the implicit call stack with a stack data structure you manage manually. For DFS on a graph:

```javascript
// Recursive DFS:
function dfs(node) {
  if (!node) return;
  visit(node);
  for (const neighbor of node.neighbors) dfs(neighbor);
}

// Iterative DFS with explicit stack:
function dfsIterative(start) {
  const stack = [start];
  while (stack.length) {
    const node = stack.pop();
    visit(node);
    for (const neighbor of node.neighbors) stack.push(neighbor);
  }
}
```

Stack overflow occurs when recursion goes too deep - typically 10,000–15,000 calls in JS. For deeply recursive problems (e.g., large trees), the iterative stack version avoids this limit.

**Bracket matching** is the canonical stack problem. The rule: when you see an opening bracket, push it. When you see a closing bracket, pop and verify it matches the most recent opening bracket. At the end, the stack must be empty (no unclosed brackets).

```javascript
function isValid(s) {
  const stack = [];
  const match = { ')': '(', ']': '[', '}': '{' };
  for (const ch of s) {
    if ('([{'.includes(ch)) {
      stack.push(ch);
    } else {
      if (stack.length === 0 || stack[stack.length - 1] !== match[ch]) return false;
      stack.pop();
    }
  }
  return stack.length === 0;
}
```

This template extends to all 'valid nesting' problems: HTML tag matching, function call balancing, and nested structure validation. The stack naturally handles the arbitrary nesting depth - the matching property is maintained regardless of how deep the nesting goes.

A **monotonic stack** is a stack that is maintained in sorted order (either always increasing from bottom to top, or always decreasing). For each new element, pop all elements that violate the sorted order before pushing the new element.

## Next Greater Element

For each element, find the first element to its right that is greater. Use a **monotonically decreasing stack** (elements decrease from bottom to top): when a new element is greater than the top, it IS the 'next greater' for the top - pop and record.

```javascript
function nextGreaterElement(arr) {
  const result = new Array(arr.length).fill(-1);
  const stack = []; // stores indices, decreasing by arr value
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    // current element is the next greater for everything smaller at the top
    while (stack.length && arr[stack[stack.length - 1]] < arr[i]) {
      result[stack.pop()] = arr[i];
    }
    stack.push(i);
  }
  return result;
}
```

## Why O(n)?

Each element is pushed exactly once and popped at most once. Total operations ≤ 2n → O(n). This is the amortized argument: each element 'pays' for its own push and pop.

## Variations

- **Previous smaller**: iterate forward, use increasing stack
- **Next smaller**: iterate forward, use increasing stack, pop when current is smaller
- **Largest rectangle in histogram**: combination of previous-smaller-left and next-smaller-right for each bar

